Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||02-12-2001||11:39 PM||||Look at the '99 &'00 dodge truck paint. You may see what you want in there. They have a purplish blue that 'appears' to change colors and hues in the darker spectrum. Looks really sweet, my uncles truck is that color and I like it alot! <BR>I'll paint one that color when I want to deviate from stock, looks like it may be an '86 GT because I don't need 2 black ones!||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||02-12-2001||11:45 PM||||Lisa, if you have a chance go to Midwestern Auto Group just 1 exit west of 270 at 161. There almost beside Crown Mercedes on 33 west (161) Go and look at the Lotus. Also check at Caldwell Chrysler on Morse rd by Northland Mall. Chrysler has some nice purplish blues, especially the minivans.||||reg||1
Z||000005||RossT||02-12-2001||11:50 PM||||That 2000 dodge blue is "patriot blue"! I posted a picture on the last thread of my caravan in patriot blue.  It does change color with the light.  In bright light it looks dark blue and turns purplish in dark condtions.  Dodge calls this a "pearl coat".  Whatever that means.  I have many more pics of my van if you want them. Most of the time it looks dark blue, but under the correct lighting it does look purlple.<P> I think that Chris Moors Northstar, also has dark blue/purple tint to it.<p>[This message has been edited by RossT (edited 02-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroLisa||02-13-2001||03:57 PM||||Thanks for you help guys,<BR>Chris Moore's northstar is the new trans am blue..no purple tint really- but I love that dark blue.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Lisa||||reg||1
Z||000007||kslish||02-13-2001||05:48 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by RossT:<BR><B>It does change color with the light.  In bright light it looks dark blue and turns purplish in dark condtions.  Dodge calls this a "pearl coat".  Whatever that means.</B><BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Maybe I'm wrong here, but isn't a "pearl coat" a layer of base coat (like solid dark blue), then a layer of translucent (or semi-transparent) "pearl coat" paint, such as purple metallic sprayed on top before finishing off with a coat of clearcoat?  <P>I'm on my way to my local Fiero club meeting tonight and I'll ask around to see if anyone knows for sure.<P>Also, maybe I'm confused....Lisa, are you looking for a paint that can "colorshift" slightly like a pearlcoat, or just a blueish-purple solid color? <P>Ken S.<BR>'87 GT and '86 SC<BR> <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||FieroLisa||02-13-2001||07:16 PM||||Well I'm not too sure on what I want. My only problem with custom or pearl is if it gets stone chips or someone hits me, it's going to be a pain to match up. So in that sense, it would be good to go with a blueish purple solid color, but then again, I would really love a pearl color (like that trans am blue color chris has, but with a red pearl over it)<P>Lisa||||reg||1
Z||000009||2xsess||02-13-2001||07:52 PM||||A pearl is small mica chips the bend the light and reflects the color of the mica. As far as Dupont and BASF goes, it's a powder form, not sure about PPG, never sold their paints.<P>Lisa, if you get one with a pearl coat, get the exact formula the paint shop uses to mix it. And if Roger is painting it for you, all he needs to do is remember how many coats of each layer he puts on the car to help lessen the hassle of color matching.<P>Roger, if you don't agree with any of what I've said that's fine as it has been a few years since I went through DuPont certified training.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Might be a but rusty<BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>AOL IM : Only2xsess<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2xsess.gif">      <BR> <A HREF="http://kresgeus.homestead.com/FieroPage.html" TARGET=_blank>Visit My Website</A>||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||02-14-2001||02:29 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 2xsess:<BR><B>A pearl is small mica chips the bend the light and reflects the color of the mica. As far as Dupont and BASF goes, it's a powder form, not sure about PPG, never sold their paints.<P>Lisa, if you get one with a pearl coat, get the exact formula the paint shop uses to mix it. And if Roger is painting it for you, all he needs to do is remember how many coats of each layer he puts on the car to help lessen the hassle of color matching.<P>Roger, if you don't agree with any of what I've said that's fine as it has been a few years since I went through DuPont certified training.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Might be a but rusty<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Your 100% rite on that, same with candy. Only thing i differ with is retouching later. The OEM colors anymore are almost impossible to match even if you use the same original can you did the car with. Temp, humidity, pressure, gun tip, viscosity all make a differnce in shades of color. You cant just paint a fender, you have to blend it to the adjoining panels. Used to be you just painted a new one and were done. now you also have to do the door and hood to get them to 'appear' to match.<P> I already checked on the flip flop redish blue Lotus. It is just a normal basecoat/clearcoat 2 stage. That did seem strange because it is a pearl. Pearls usually are 3.... or even 4 stage with a tinted clear over the pearl and under the clear topcoat. Thats whats on candy red mustangs. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||1FST2M6||02-14-2001||08:46 AM||||thier pearl is in both the base and clear... (thats what Lotus America and my friends at the Lotus/Alfa dealer told me when i was researching Chrome Orange)  <A HREF="http://www.paulspruell.com" TARGET=_blank>www.paulspruell.com</A>   ||||reg||1
Z||000012||rogergarrison||02-14-2001||12:26 PM||||that makes sense fst, sorta like Maacos integrated clear.||||reg||1
